# Security & Defensive Engineering Guardrails

## Zero-Trust & Access Control
- Enforce strict server-side authentication and authorization on all non-public endpoints. Never rely on UI client-side route guards alone.
- Validate object-level permissions (prevent Insecure Direct Object References - IDOR): ensure the authenticated user actually owns or is authorized to access the requested resource ID.
- Hash passwords using modern cryptographic standards (`bcrypt` with cost >= 12 or `Argon2id`). Never store plain-text or MD5/SHA1 passwords.

## Injection & Attack Surface Prevention
- Eliminate SQL Injection: use parameterized queries or ORM bindings exclusively. Never concatenate raw user input into SQL queries.
- Sanitize and escape all dynamic user content before rendering to eliminate Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).
- Implement Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) with strict origin allowlists. Never use `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on authenticated APIs.
- Protect state-mutating endpoints with CSRF tokens or SameSite cookie configurations.

## Secret Hygiene & Data Protection
- NEVER hardcode secrets, API keys, private certificates, or database passwords in source code or commit histories.
- Use environment variables (`.env`) loaded through centralized config providers. Verify `.env` is listed in `.gitignore`.
- Sanitize and redact Personally Identifiable Information (PII), passwords, and access tokens before emitting logs.
